{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788355348,"nonce":"9zYpkNGHasfoHallY0JXB+Uy0pc=","message":"**PARKED — `state:blocked`. Orchestrator ruling, repair round 2026-09-02.** The park condition holds, but **the premise I filed this issue with was wrong**, and the correction narrows the question to something Evan can settle in one sentence.\n\n\nI wrote that `Room.ID` \"is assigned on rebuild, and `Map.MapUpdate` calls `TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms` every frame, so 'room 52' is not the same handle across days.\" The call happens every frame. **The rebuild does not.** `Verse/RegionAndRoomUpdater.cs`, verified in the decompile:\n\n```csharp\npublic void TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms() {\n  if (working || !Enabled) return;\n  working = true;\n  if (!initialized) RebuildAllRegionsAndRooms();\n  if (!map.regionDirtyer.AnyDirty) { working = false; return; }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n`RegionDirtyer.AnyDirty` is `dirtyCells.Count \u003e 0`, so an undisturbed map early-returns; where cells are dirty, `CreateOrAttachToExistingRooms` reuses the existing `Room` via `FindCurrentRoomNeighborWithMostRegions`. **`Room.ID` is stable across ordinary play.** What destroys it is a **LOAD**: `!initialized` takes `RebuildAllRegionsAndRooms()`, every room is re-made, and `nextRoomID` is not scribed. Run `m1-20260901` spanned bench relaunches, which is why its ids moved. Recorded in DESIGN's decisions log at `7fbe7db`.\n\n\n**A durable handle already ships.** `Spatial.cs`'s `LandmarkComponent` is a `GameComponent` **with** `ExposeData`, scribing named cells, and `templates/freezer-kitchen.md` already tells the agent to register one. I checked. The identity problem I filed does not exist.\n\n\n**The BASELINE, not the handle.** \"This room used to be a Barracks\" is a remembered prior state, and remembering it across a load is new scribed state. This project rules that out for a worker — `d16a463` is an open hazard on observers writing scribed state, and this round's `f9dadc7` ruling refused the same trade for stall history. So:\n\n- **Option A — extend the already-scribed `LandmarkComponent`** to carry a role baseline per landmark. Cheapest by far, since the component, its `ExposeData` and its save-compat story already exist and are already trusted. Cost: it grows the one piece of scribed state the mod owns, and the reason `f9dadc7` was refused the same thing was that nobody had ruled on it.\n- **Option B — no baseline; report only transitions observed within one session.** Zero new scribed state. Cost: a role regression that happens across a relaunch is invisible, which is precisely the case that occurred in `m1-20260901`. That makes it a detector that misses the only instance we have.\n- **Option C — park item 1 indefinitely and ship item 2 alone** (below).\n\n**This is Evan's call**, because it is a question about what state the mod is allowed to own, not about how to write the check.\n\n\n\"A destroyed building of consequence is reported\" needs **no room identity at all** — a Harmony destruction postfix of the same shape as the five already in `JournalHooks.cs` closes it with zero persistence. That half could ship in an afternoon.\n\n**Deliberately not filed as a separate issue.** The split changes this issue's contract and the round is scoped to its six-item acceptance list; filing a third issue to hold one cheap half would add ledger noise for no gain. A future session taking this issue should read this comment first and ship item 2 immediately regardless of how Evan rules on the baseline.\n\n\nA detector keyed on `Room.ID` diffing, which is what the body asked for. It was abandoned once the ID's actual lifetime was established: it would have been correct across play and wrong across exactly the event that produced the reported symptom, while looking correct in testing.","files":null}]}